module dev.slatelang.slate

// What the lexer answers with: a kind, and nothing else.
//
// **A token carries no payload, and that is a decision rather than an omission.** The name in an
// identifier, the digits in a number and the bytes in a string are all *already* in the input, and
// every token comes with the `Span` that says where. So a variant carrying a `string` would be a
// second copy of text the parser can read back whenever it wants it, and the enum would stop being
// a bare one -- which costs both of the things the compiler gives a bare enum for free: `==`, and a
// `Display` that names the variant. Reading the lexeme back off the source is what Clang and rustc
// do for the same reason.
//
// The consequence to know while reading `parse.sysl`: **decoding a literal is the parser's job**, not
// the lexer's. The lexer proves a literal is well formed and says where it is; the parser opens a
// scanner on that span when it needs the value.
// `Eq` is absent from the clause on purpose: a simple enum is one already, its value being its
// discriminant, and asking for it is refused in as many words.
enum Tok derives Display
    // Literals. The span is the whole lexeme, quotes included for a string.
    //
    // **The `Lit` suffixes say what these are, and are not there to dodge a clash.** Two enums may
    // carry a variant of one name -- `sh.sysl.parsing`'s `Number` also has `Int` and `Real`, and
    // this file imports that package wholesale -- because the expected type is what picks between
    // them, and where nothing supplies one the compiler says so and asks for `Tok.Int`. What the
    // suffix buys is a reader who can tell a token that *is* a number from the number itself.
    IntLit
    RealLit
    StrLit
    Ident

    // Words. `end` is deliberately absent -- see the note below.
    Val
    Var
    If
    Then
    Elif
    Else
    While
    For
    In
    Loop
    Do
    Match
    Break
    Continue
    Return
    True
    False
    Nil

    // Grouping and separators.
    LParen
    RParen
    LBracket
    RBracket
    LBrace
    RBrace
    Comma
    Colon
    Dot
    Arrow

    // `...`, which gathers what is left of an array in a pattern. Read before `.`, or it would be
    // three field selections.
    Spread

    // `|`, which separates the alternatives of a pattern. Read after `||`.
    Pipe

    // `@`, which binds the whole of what a pattern matched as well as its parts.
    At

    // Operators.
    Assign
    Plus
    Minus
    Star
    Slash
    Percent
    EqEq
    BangEq
    Lt
    LtEq
    Gt
    GtEq
    AndAnd
    OrOr
    Bang

    // Structure, supplied by the layout pass rather than by any character in the file.
    Newline
    Indent
    Dedent
    Eof
end Tok

// **`end` is not a keyword and must not become one.** sysl treats it as a *soft* word -- an ordinary
// identifier everywhere except immediately before a construct keyword -- so that a program may still
// call a variable `end`. Slate follows that, which means the lexer emits `Ident` for it and the
// parser decides by what comes next. Making it a `Tok` of its own would be the easy thing and would
// take the name away from every program that wanted it.

// Whether a token can begin an expression.
//
// This is what lets the parser tell a *definition* from a *call* without backtracking, and it is the
// one piece of lookahead slate's grammar needs.
starts_expr(t: Tok) -> bool
    t match
        IntLit -> true
        RealLit -> true
        StrLit -> true
        Ident -> true
        True -> true
        False -> true
        Nil -> true
        If -> true
        LParen -> true
        LBracket -> true
        LBrace -> true
        Minus -> true
        Bang -> true
        _ -> false
end starts_expr
